import Mathlib.Logic.Relation
import Mettapedia.Languages.ProcessCalculi.ConstraintMultisetRewriting.RuleSyntax.FirstOrderEq
import Mettapedia.Languages.ProcessCalculi.ConstraintMultisetRewriting.MeTTaOperationsComm
import Mettapedia.Languages.ProcessCalculi.MeTTaCalculus.CorrectedReflection

/-!
# The pattern frame modulo the bag laws

The manuscript's structural equivalence on processes is the bag laws of parallel composition,
`P | 0 ≡ P`, `P | Q ≡ Q | P`, `P | (Q | R) ≡ (P | Q) | R`, with alpha-equivalence. The corrected semantics
reads names as compared modulo the structural equivalence of the processes they quote (the name
equivalence of the reflective higher-order calculus). The one frame of the finite hosting
(`OneFrame.frames patternAction`) compares terms syntactically. Here the same terms and the same action of
structural substitution are compared **modulo the bag laws**: rewriting modulo the structural congruence.

## The relation

* `BagAxiom`: the laws at the root, on the n-ary bags of the syntax (`pPar`): reordering, flattening a
  nested bag, dropping `0`, a bag of one member is that member, the empty bag is `0`.
* `BagRewrite`: one law at one position. Every constructor is a context: a law may act inside a name, a
  pattern, a channel, a continuation or a quoted process.
* `BagLaws`: the equivalence closure of `BagRewrite`, i.e. the congruence generated by the laws.

## What is shown

* **Substitution respects the relation** (`bagLaws_psubst`), for every pair of terms, unknowns included, by
  induction on the derivation: instances of the laws are laws, and substitution commutes with contexts.
* **The canonical form** `bagCanon` (every parallel bag flattened, cleared of `0`, sorted by the injective
  structural code `patternCode` through `codeOrder`, unwrapped when it has one member) decides the relation
  on all terms: `bagLaws_iff : BagLaws s t ↔ bagCanon s = bagCanon t`. So at every solution
  `σ`, an equation holds modulo the bag laws exactly when the canonical forms of its two sides read through
  `σ` are equal (`solves_iff`). Canonicalizing before or after a substitution agrees
  (`bagCanon_psubst_bagCanon`).
* **The quotient frame** `QX = OneFrame.frames bagAction`, with `bagAction` the substitution action of
  `patternAction` with `BagLaws` as its equality, and its extensions `QE`.

## Deep, not shallow

`BagEq` (the corrected semantics' equivalence) acts on the top-level parallel components of a closed
process and compares each component syntactically after closing: it covers the bag laws at the top level
and alpha-equivalence, and nothing inside a component. It is not closed under quoting
(`bagEq_not_closed_under_quote`): `(a | b)` and `(b | a)` are related, the names `@(a | b)` and `@(b | a)`
are not. Comparing names modulo the structural equivalence of the processes they quote is closure under the
quote constructor (`bagLaws_quote`), and names occur in patterns, channels, continuations and quoted
processes, so the relation must be closed under every constructor that can surround a name: a congruence.
`BagLaws` is deep. On closed forms `BagEq` is contained in it (`bagLaws_close_of_bagEq`);
alpha-equivalence is not (`bagEq_not_bagLaws`), and it cannot be added to an equality of this frame, because
closing does not commute with substitution (`close_not_substitution_stable`: a substitution captures under a
binder).

No solver is claimed here: the frame's admission stays semantic.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace Mettapedia.Languages.ProcessCalculi.ConstraintMultisetRewriting.RuleSyntax.StepWeb.ReflQuotient

